I don't have a problem with your definitions of cheat and abuse,
although I might expand upon 'cheat' to include some of 'abuse'. 

Personally I'm not really down with the concept of abusing. Abusing is
either cheating or it's legitimate, to me there is no real in between,
at least in Nethack. 

An example of cheating is the lodestone bug. Using this bug was
cheating since enabled you to get infinite wishes as soon as you hit
the Castle.

But unless you're using a BUG to do something that is impossible
otherwise impossible, I don't really see the value in talking about
it. 

If for personal honor you insist on playing without doing X, fine, but
honestly it's not very important to me. The reason I feel this way is
because there is SO MUCH in Nethack that is abusable on minor levels
that trying to pick through it and decide what's fair game and what's
not fair game is just pointless. 

A good example is having a pet steal stuff from shops. Having your pet
steal stuff from shops is incredibly stupid and makes almost
meaningless the one minor advantage healers+tourists have (a fat
pocketful of cash).

Yet it's possible, and it's fixable, and it's not fixed. It's there
because it's part of what makes the game interesting. Abuse is a
fundamental part of nethack.

To me it's not about what the devteam means, it's about what is
obviously fair and what is obviously not fair. If you lack the moral
compass to determine what's fair and what's not fair, no set of rules
is going to encourage you to play fair. 

